# rules: kotlin-webview-javascript-interface
# languages: kotlin
# fixture: kotlin/CWE-749.kt
# from: CWE-749 (Exposed Dangerous Method or Function)
#
# `addJavascriptInterface` puts a Kotlin object on the other side of a bridge
# from the JavaScript in the page, and the page decides what to call. That is
# the point of it, and it is only safe while the app chose the page. A
# redirect, an injected script or a man-in-the-middle on a cleartext load puts
# somebody else on the other end.
#
# So the bridge on its own is not the finding, twice over.
#
# A file that never loads anything into a WebView is a file where this is
# somebody else's problem - a base class, a test double - and the rule stays
# out of it. That test is the file rather than the view, because a WebView is
# configured in one method and loaded in another often enough that asking it of
# a single receiver would answer no almost everywhere.
#
# And a method that names `file:///android_asset/` or `file:///android_res/` is
# showing HTML the app shipped, which is the case `addJavascriptInterface` is
# actually for. The test there is the method's own text, and it is the coarse
# one on purpose: a method that loads an asset and a remote page both goes
# unreported, and quiet is the right direction to be coarse in.

["$V.addJavascriptInterface($O, $N)"] as $bridges
| ["$V.loadUrl($U)", "$V.loadUrl($U, $$$_)", "$V.loadData($U, $$$_)",
   "$V.loadDataWithBaseURL($U, $$$_)"] as $loads
| ["fun $F($$$_) { $BODY }"] as $methods
| "\"file:///android_(?:asset|res)/" as $ownContent
| scan_ast("*.kt"; $bridges + $loads + $methods) as $all
| ( $all | of($bridges)
    | in_files_with($all | of($loads))
    | outside($all | of($methods) | where_capture("BODY"; $ownContent)) )
| finding("kotlin-webview-javascript-interface";
    "$O is reachable from the JavaScript in whatever page this view loads, and "
    + "what it loads is not written out here")
| report
